---
name: code-writing-style
description: Use for ANY coding task from start to finish — new features, bug fixes, refactors, and reviews. One complete workflow covering design approval, SOLID/DRY principles, root-cause debugging, simplification, and the merge-quality gate. Use before writing code, while designing a class or function, when something breaks, when working code feels messy, and before anything is considered done.
---

# Code Writing Style

Complete end-to-end coding workflow in five parts. Each part owns a phase;
together they cover a task from "someone asks for something" to "the change
is merged." No part repeats another's content — cross-references point to
the part that owns a topic instead of restating it.

**How the parts fit together:**

```
Part 1: BRAINSTORMING       → turn a request into an approved design
         (design/approval gate)
              │
              ▼
Part 2: SOLID + DRY DESIGN  → shape that design correctly, avoid duplication
         (used DURING Part 1's design and DURING Part 3's implementation)
              │
              ▼
        [implement]
              │
      ┌───────┴────────┐
      ▼                ▼
Part 3: DEBUGGING    (code works, but is messy)
   (something broke)       │
      │                    ▼
      │             Part 4: SIMPLIFICATION
      │                    │
      └──────────┬─────────┘
                  ▼
         Part 5: CODE REVIEW
         (the merge gate — everything passes through here)
```

## Part 0: Routing — Which Part Applies

Check this before writing, editing, or deleting any code. State in one line
which part you're starting with.

| Task looks like... | Start at | Then |
|---|---|---|
| "Build X", "add a feature", "create a...", any new capability | **Part 1** | Approved → Part 2 informs the design → implement → Part 5 |
| "Fix this bug", failing test, exception, unexpected output, "why does X happen" | **Part 3** | Root cause found → minimal fix → Part 5 |
| "This works but is messy/hard to read/too complex" | **Part 4** | After simplifying → Part 5 |
| "Review this", "is this good", ready to merge | **Part 5** | Findings addressed → re-review if Critical/Required |
| Structuring a class/module, choosing a pattern, a function growing extra parameters | **Part 2** | Feeds back into whichever part is active — not a standalone entry point |
| Pure question, read-only investigation | none | Answer directly; re-check this table the moment it becomes a code change |

**Ordering rules when tasks overlap:**

1. Design gates (Part 1) come before design content (Part 2) — Part 2 informs what gets proposed inside Part 1's gate, it doesn't bypass it.
2. A bug found mid-implementation or mid-review routes to Part 3, not back to Part 1 — root-cause and fix the specific defect, then resume.
3. A structural finding during Part 5 routes to Part 2's remedies or GoF table, not back to Part 1 — unless it's large enough to be a genuine redesign (see rule 4).
4. Hidden complexity discovered mid-task upgrades the design path (see Part 1's ratchet rule) — stop, say so, re-run this table.
5. Part 4 never substitutes for Part 5 — simplifying is optional pre-review cleanup; every change still goes through the merge gate.
6. 3+ failed fix attempts in Part 3 pause debugging for an architecture discussion (Part 3, Phase 4.5) — this may route back to Part 1 if the conclusion is a genuine redesign.

---

# Part 1: Brainstorming — Design & Approval

**Owns:** turning an idea into an approved design before any implementation starts.

<HARD-GATE>
Do NOT invoke Part 2's implementation consequences, write code, scaffold a
project, or take any implementation action until you have told the human
what you intend and they have approved it. This applies to every task on
every path below — the ceremony scales with the task; the approval gate
never does.
</HARD-GATE>

## Three Paths

Classify the request out loud before the first question, so the human can
override it:

- **Spike** — a feasibility question ("can we...", "is it possible...")
  whose output is an answer, not code you keep. Present the question and
  probe plan in 2-3 sentences, get a nod, investigate as cheaply as
  correctness allows, report findings. No design doc.
- **Bounded** — a well-scoped change to code that already exists in this
  repo: a new flag, a small endpoint, a one-file fix. If there's no
  existing flow to change, it's not bounded. Ask clarifying questions,
  present a short design IN CHAT (a few sentences to a few short
  paragraphs), STOP, get an explicit yes, then implement via the normal
  workflow (Parts 2-5 as applicable). No spec file.
- **Architectural** — new projects, new subsystems, changes that
  restructure how components fit together or alter interfaces others
  depend on. Full process below.

When in doubt between two paths, take the heavier one. The ratchet is
one-way: hidden complexity discovered mid-task upgrades the path — stop,
say so, step up. Nothing downgrades mid-task.

**Anti-pattern:** "Too simple to need approval." Every path ends with the
human approving intent before implementation. A todo list, a single-function
utility, a config change — the design may be two sentences in chat, but it
still needs to be presented and approved.

## Checklist by Path

**Spike:** explore context → present question + probe (2-3 sentences) →
get approval → investigate → report recommendation (label anything built
as throwaway).

**Bounded:** explore context (files, docs, recent commits) → ask
clarifying questions one at a time → present short design in chat
(approach, files touched, testing) → STOP for explicit yes → implement
(normal workflow, TDD applies, no plan document).

**Architectural:**
1. Explore project context
2. Offer the visual companion just-in-time (see below) — never upfront
3. Ask clarifying questions one at a time — purpose, constraints, success criteria
4. Propose 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, lead with your recommendation
5. Present design in sections scaled to complexity (a few sentences to 200-300 words each), get approval after each section
6. Write design doc to `docs/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`, commit it
7. Spec self-review (see below), fix inline
8. Human reviews the written spec
9. Transition to implementation (Parts 2-5)

**If the project is too large for a single spec** (multiple independent
subsystems), flag this immediately and help decompose into sub-projects
before refining details of any one piece. Each sub-project gets its own
cycle through this whole workflow.

## Exploring Approaches (architectural path)

Propose 2-3 approaches conversationally, trade-offs and your recommendation
first. YAGNI ruthlessly — remove unnecessary features from every approach.

## Presenting the Design

Cover architecture, components, data flow, error handling, testing. Ask
after each section whether it looks right. Design for isolation: each unit
should have one clear purpose, a well-defined interface, and be
understandable without reading its internals — if a change to one unit's
internals breaks its consumers, or you can't say what it does without
reading it, the boundaries need work (this is Part 2's Single
Responsibility Principle applied at the module level — see Part 2 rather
than re-deriving it here).

**Working in existing codebases:** explore structure before proposing
changes, follow existing patterns. If existing code has problems that
affect this work (a file too large, tangled responsibilities), include
targeted improvements in the design — but don't propose unrelated
refactoring. Stay focused on what serves the current goal.

## Spec Self-Review (architectural path)

After writing the spec, check with fresh eyes:

1. **Placeholder scan** — any "TBD", "TODO", incomplete sections, vague requirements? Fix them.
2. **Internal consistency** — do sections contradict each other?
3. **Scope check** — focused enough for one implementation plan, or does it need decomposition?
4. **Ambiguity check** — could any requirement be read two ways? Pick one, make it explicit.

Fix issues inline, no need to re-review — fix and move on.

## Visual Companion

A browser-based companion for mockups, diagrams, and visual options. Not a
mode — a tool. Offer it **just-in-time**, never upfront: the first time a
question would genuinely be clearer shown than described, offer it as its
own message with nothing else in it:

> "This next part might be easier if I show you — I can put together
> mockups, diagrams, and comparisons in a browser tab as we go. Want me to?"

Per question after acceptance, decide: would the human understand this
better by seeing it than reading it? Visual → mockups, wireframes, layout
comparisons, architecture diagrams. Terminal → requirements questions,
conceptual choices, tradeoff lists, scope decisions. A UI *topic* is not
automatically a visual *question* — "what does personality mean in this
context" is conceptual (terminal); "which wizard layout works better" is
visual (browser).

## Red Flags (Part 1)

| Thought | Reality |
|---|---|
| "This is too simple to need a design" | Simple means a short design, not no design. |
| "I'll call it bounded and skip the spec" | Reaching for a label to skip work IS the doubt — take the heavier path. |
| "It's bounded, I'll start while they read the design" | The gate is the approval, not the design's length. Stop until you hear yes. |
| "The spike works, I'll keep the code" | A spike's output is an answer. Keeping the code is a new request — classify it. |
| "It grew, but I'm almost done" | Hidden complexity upgrades the path mid-task. Stop and say so. |

---

# Part 2: SOLID + DRY Design

**Owns:** where responsibility should live, when to extract vs. duplicate,
and which design pattern (if any) fits. Used *during* Part 1's design and
*during* implementation — not a standalone entry point.

## When to Use

Starting a new class/module/function; a function picking up a third+
parameter (especially booleans); logic that "feels familiar"; extending a
class with `isinstance`/`type ==` branches instead of polymorphism;
structuring a feature with several variants (payment methods, notification
channels, export formats).

**When not to:** prototypes/spikes (Part 1) before they graduate to real
code; a codebase small enough that abstraction costs more than the
duplication it prevents; before you understand the problem well enough to
know the right boundaries (write the concrete version first, abstract from
2-3 real examples, not from imagination).

## The Five Principles (SOLID)

### S — Single Responsibility

**One reason to change.** *Trigger:* you describe a class using "and"; you
edit a class for a business-logic reason and also touch its formatting,
persistence, or logging in the same edit.

```python
# VIOLATES: parsing, validation, AND persistence in one class
class OrderProcessor:
    def process(self, raw_order: str):
        data = json.loads(raw_order)
        if not data.get("items"):
            raise ValueError("Order must have items")
        conn = sqlite3.connect("orders.db")
        conn.execute("INSERT INTO orders VALUES (?)", (raw_order,))
        conn.commit()
        return data

# FOLLOWS: each class owns one reason to change
class OrderParser:
    def parse(self, raw_order: str) -> dict:
        return json.loads(raw_order)

class OrderValidator:
    def validate(self, order: dict) -> None:
        if not order.get("items"):
            raise ValueError("Order must have items")

class OrderRepository:
    def save(self, order: dict) -> None:
        conn = sqlite3.connect("orders.db")
        conn.execute("INSERT INTO orders VALUES (?)", (json.dumps(order),))
        conn.commit()

class OrderProcessor:
    def __init__(self, parser, validator, repository):
        self._parser, self._validator, self._repository = parser, validator, repository

    def process(self, raw_order: str) -> dict:
        order = self._parser.parse(raw_order)
        self._validator.validate(order)
        self._repository.save(order)
        return order
```

Don't over-apply: a five-line function that parses *and* returns a value
isn't two responsibilities. SRP is about independent axes of change, not
line count.

```cpp
// VIOLATES SRP: parsing, validation, AND persistence in one class
class OrderProcessor {
public:
    Order process(const std::string& raw_order) {
        Order order = parse(raw_order);              // parsing
        if (order.items.empty())                      // validation
            throw std::invalid_argument("Order must have items");
        std::ofstream out("orders.db", std::ios::app); // persistence
        out << raw_order << "\n";
        return order;
    }
private:
    Order parse(const std::string& raw_order) { /* ... */ }
};

// FOLLOWS SRP: each class owns one reason to change
class OrderParser {
public:
    Order parse(const std::string& raw_order) { /* ... */ }
};

class OrderValidator {
public:
    void validate(const Order& order) {
        if (order.items.empty())
            throw std::invalid_argument("Order must have items");
    }
};

class OrderRepository {
public:
    void save(const Order& order) {
        std::ofstream out("orders.db", std::ios::app);
        out << order.serialize() << "\n";
    }
};

class OrderProcessor {
public:
    OrderProcessor(OrderParser& parser, OrderValidator& validator, OrderRepository& repo)
        : parser_(parser), validator_(validator), repo_(repo) {}

    Order process(const std::string& raw_order) {
        Order order = parser_.parse(raw_order);
        validator_.validate(order);
        repo_.save(order);
        return order;
    }
private:
    OrderParser& parser_;
    OrderValidator& validator_;
    OrderRepository& repo_;
};
```

### O — Open/Closed

**Open for extension, closed for modification.** *Trigger:* an
`if/elif`/`switch` chain branching on type/category, where every new case
means editing that function again.

```typescript
// VIOLATES: every new shape means editing calculateArea again
function calculateArea(shape: { kind: string; [k: string]: any }): number {
  if (shape.kind === "circle") return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
  if (shape.kind === "rectangle") return shape.width * shape.height;
  throw new Error(`Unknown shape: ${shape.kind}`);
}

// FOLLOWS: new shapes are added, existing code is untouched
interface Shape { area(): number; }
class Circle implements Shape {
  constructor(private radius: number) {}
  area() { return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2; }
}
class Rectangle implements Shape {
  constructor(private width: number, private height: number) {}
  area() { return this.width * this.height; }
}
function totalArea(shapes: Shape[]): number {
  return shapes.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s.area(), 0);
}
```

Don't over-apply: a two-branch `if/else` that will realistically never grow
a third case doesn't need a hierarchy. OCP earns its cost when new cases
are a recurring, expected kind of change (see GoF table below).

```cpp
// VIOLATES OCP: every new shape means editing calculateArea again
double calculateArea(const std::string& kind, double a, double b) {
    if (kind == "circle") return M_PI * a * a;
    if (kind == "rectangle") return a * b;
    throw std::invalid_argument("Unknown shape: " + kind);
}

// FOLLOWS OCP: new shapes are added, existing code is untouched
class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() const = 0;
    virtual ~Shape() = default;
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    explicit Circle(double radius) : radius_(radius) {}
    double area() const override { return M_PI * radius_ * radius_; }
private:
    double radius_;
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    Rectangle(double width, double height) : width_(width), height_(height) {}
    double area() const override { return width_ * height_; }
private:
    double width_, height_;
};

// Adding Triangle later touches zero existing code.
double totalArea(const std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Shape>>& shapes) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (const auto& s : shapes) sum += s->area();
    return sum;
}
```

### L — Liskov Substitution

**A subtype must be usable anywhere its base type is expected, without the
caller needing to know which one it got.** *Trigger:* `isinstance(x,
SpecificSubclass)` checks before calling a method, or a subclass overriding
a method to raise `NotImplementedError` for cases the base type promised
to handle.

```python
# VIOLATES: Penguin breaks the Bird contract callers rely on
class Bird:
    def fly(self): return "flying"
class Penguin(Bird):
    def fly(self): raise NotImplementedError("Penguins can't fly")

# FOLLOWS: the base type only promises what every subtype can do
class Bird:
    def move(self): raise NotImplementedError
class FlyingBird(Bird):
    def move(self): return "flying"
class Penguin(Bird):
    def move(self): return "swimming"
```

If callers need `isinstance` checks to use your hierarchy safely, the
hierarchy is wrong — fix the type boundary, don't add more checks.

### I — Interface Segregation

**Don't force a class to depend on methods it doesn't use.** *Trigger:* an
interface where some implementers stub out methods with `pass` or
`NotImplementedError`.

```typescript
// VIOLATES: a read-only report forced to implement write()
interface DataSource { read(): string; write(data: string): void; delete(): void; }
class ReadOnlyReport implements DataSource {
  read() { return "report data"; }
  write(_: string): void { throw new Error("Not supported"); }
  delete(): void { throw new Error("Not supported"); }
}

// FOLLOWS: split by capability, implement only what applies
interface Readable { read(): string; }
interface Writable { write(data: string): void; }
interface Deletable { delete(): void; }
class ReadOnlyReport implements Readable {
  read() { return "report data"; }
}
```

### D — Dependency Inversion

**Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.** *Trigger:* a
business-logic class directly instantiates concrete infrastructure (a
specific DB driver, HTTP client, file writer) instead of receiving an
interface.

```python
# VIOLATES: OrderService is hard-wired to PostgresDatabase
class PostgresDatabase:
    def save(self, record: dict) -> None: ...
class OrderService:
    def __init__(self):
        self.db = PostgresDatabase()   # can't test, can't swap

# FOLLOWS: OrderService depends on an abstraction
from typing import Protocol
class Database(Protocol):
    def save(self, record: dict) -> None: ...
class OrderService:
    def __init__(self, db: Database):
        self.db = db   # Postgres, SQLite, or a test double all work

    def place_order(self, order: dict):
        self.db.save(order)
```

Don't over-apply: injecting an interface for something with exactly one
implementation that will only ever have one is ceremony without payoff.
DIP earns its cost at real seams — I/O, external services, anything you'll
swap or fake in tests.

```cpp
// VIOLATES DIP: OrderService is hard-wired to PostgresDatabase
class PostgresDatabase {
public:
    void save(const Order& record) { /* ... */ }
};
class OrderService {
public:
    OrderService() : db_(std::make_unique<PostgresDatabase>()) {}  // can't swap, can't mock
private:
    std::unique_ptr<PostgresDatabase> db_;
};

// FOLLOWS DIP: OrderService depends on an abstraction
class Database {
public:
    virtual void save(const Order& record) = 0;
    virtual ~Database() = default;
};
class OrderService {
public:
    explicit OrderService(std::shared_ptr<Database> db) : db_(std::move(db)) {}
    void placeOrder(const Order& order) { db_->save(order); }
private:
    std::shared_ptr<Database> db_;   // Postgres, SQLite, or a test double all work
};
```

**C-specific note (no classes/interfaces):** the same principle applies via
function pointers or a `struct` of function pointers passed in, rather than
a module calling a hardcoded function directly:

```c
/* VIOLATES DIP: order_service.c calls postgres_save() directly — can't test, can't swap */
void place_order(Order *order) {
    postgres_save(order);
}

/* FOLLOWS DIP: the save function is passed in, not hardcoded */
typedef struct {
    void (*save)(const Order *order);
} Database;

void place_order(const Database *db, Order *order) {
    db->save(order);   /* Postgres, SQLite, or a test stub all work */
}
```

## DRY, Done Right

**DRY is about knowledge, not code that looks similar.** This is the
single most common misapplication — over-applying DRY produces its own
bloat: shared helpers serving unrelated callers that happen to look alike
today and will diverge tomorrow.

```python
# These LOOK like duplication...
def validate_username(name: str) -> bool:
    return 3 <= len(name) <= 20
def validate_product_code(code: str) -> bool:
    return 3 <= len(code) <= 20

# ...but merging them is WRONG — different business rules that happen
# to share a number today. A username's length rule and a product code's
# length rule are different knowledge that will change for different
# reasons. Merging means one change silently breaks the other's rule.
```

Compare: two places computing someone's age from a birth date **are** the
same knowledge — that belongs in one function, because when the
definition changes, it must change in exactly one place for both callers.

**The test:** if this logic changes, should every place it's copied change
*for the same reason, at the same time*? Yes → extract it. No (same shape,
different reason to change) → leave it duplicated.

### The Rule of Three

Don't extract after the *first* duplication; be cautious even after the
second. Wait for a genuine third occurrence — or high confidence the
second is the same knowledge — before creating a shared function, base
class, or module. Extracting from one example produces an abstraction
shaped like that one example, which usually needs reshaping the moment a
different second case arrives.

## Duplication-Prevention Checklist

The mechanical step that makes "avoid duplication" actionable — run this
**before** writing a new function, class, or module:

```
1. SEARCH FIRST
   - grep/search the codebase for the domain term before writing a new
     implementation
   - Check for an existing utils/, helpers/, common/, shared/ module
   - Check the module that owns this concept

2. IF SOMETHING SIMILAR EXISTS, ASK:
   - Same knowledge, or coincidental resemblance? (DRY test above)
   - Can the existing function be reused as-is, or with a parameter
     (without becoming a boolean-flag grab bag)?
   - Does it need a genuine variant — overload, subtype, or strategy
     (see GoF table), not a copy?

3. IF NOTHING EXISTS:
   - First occurrence → write the concrete version inline, don't
     pre-abstract (YAGNI, below)
   - Second occurrence of near-identical logic → note it, extract only
     if confident it's the same knowledge
   - Third occurrence → extract now (Rule of Three)

4. NAME IT FOR WHAT IT MEANS, NOT WHERE IT'S USED
   - calculateShippingCost(), not calculateCost2() or
     calculateCostForCheckoutPage()
   - A name scoped to one call site signals the function is about to be
     duplicated at the next call site instead of reused
```

### The "Bloated Function" Smell

Duplication and bloat are two sides of one coin — avoiding duplication by
stuffing every variant into one function creates the opposite problem.

```typescript
// BLOATED: avoided duplication by adding a flag for every variant
function sendNotification(
  message: string, isUrgent: boolean, isEmail: boolean,
  isSms: boolean, skipQuietHours: boolean, retryOnFailure: boolean,
) { /* 80 lines branching on every flag combination */ }

// SIMPLE: one function per channel, shared core for real shared knowledge
interface Notifier { send(message: string, options: { urgent: boolean }): void; }
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { send(message, options) { /* ... */ } }
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier { send(message, options) { /* ... */ } }
```

A function with three or more boolean parameters is almost always hiding
distinct behaviors that should be separate functions, a strategy, or a
clearly-named options object — not more flags on the pile.

## YAGNI: The Boundary

**You Aren't Gonna Need It.** Every principle above is as much a reason to
*not* write something yet as a reason to extract it now:

- Don't build a plugin system for one plugin
- Don't add a config option nobody asked for "in case it's needed"
- Don't create an interface with one implementation and no planned second
- Don't add a generic `options: Record<string, any>` "for flexibility" —
  that defers duplication-of-effort onto every future caller

SOLID tells you **where** a responsibility should live once it exists.
YAGNI tells you **whether** it should exist yet. Build the concrete thing
for the case in front of you, shaped so a real second case is easy to add
(open/closed) — but don't build the extension point speculatively.

## GoF Pattern Decision Table

Go symptom → pattern, never pattern → excuse. A pattern around a single
concrete case is pure indirection with no payoff.

| Symptom | Pattern | Why it fits | Common misuse |
|---|---|---|---|
| Object creation duplicated across call sites, or the concrete class depends on a runtime condition | **Factory** | Centralizes "which concrete class" in one place | Wrapping a single `new X()` "for consistency" |
| A family of interchangeable behaviors swapped at runtime (sort order, pricing, payment method) | **Strategy** | Each variant is its own class behind one interface; adding one doesn't touch others | Using it for two variants that will never become three — an `if/else` is simpler |
| Multiple parts must react to one object's state changes, subject shouldn't know who's listening | **Observer** | Decouples event source from consumers | Using it for a single fixed listener — a direct call is simpler |
| An existing interface doesn't match what calling code expects (often 3rd-party) | **Adapter** | Isolates the mismatch in one place | Adapting your own code to itself — change one side instead |
| Behavior added to individual objects at runtime without subclassing every combination | **Decorator** | Avoids a combinatorial explosion of subclasses | Using it when a simple optional parameter would do |
| A complex subsystem needs one simple entry point for common cases | **Facade** | Small, stable surface over volatile internals | Making it the *only* way in, blocking power users from subsystem detail |
| A tree of part-whole hierarchies needs uniform treatment | **Composite** | Leaf and branch treated identically | Forcing it onto a flat list that will never nest |
| Several classes share an algorithm but differ in specific steps | **Template Method** | Shared skeleton in one place, subclasses override only what varies | Using it when "shared" is only shared by two classes and already diverging |
| Construction has many optional parameters, invalid partial states possible | **Builder** | Readable construction, prevents invalid intermediate states | Building one for a 2-3 field object — named args are simpler |
| Exactly one instance must exist, global access genuinely required | **Singleton** | Rarely — see note | Nearly always reached out of habit; hides a dependency instead of declaring it (violates DIP). Prefer passing the instance explicitly. |

**On Singleton specifically:** last resort, not a default. Shared
config/connection pools are usually better as one object constructed once
at startup and passed to whatever needs it.

## Red Flags (Part 2)

- Function with 3+ boolean parameters
- `isinstance`/`typeof`/`instanceof` branching that should have been polymorphism
- `new ConcreteClass()` inside business logic with no test double possible
- Interface where implementers throw `NotImplementedError` for unsupported methods
- Writing a new helper without searching for an existing one first
- A shared "utils" function growing feature-specific branches per caller
- A pattern introduced with only one concrete case in sight
- A Singleton introduced for convenience, not a genuine single-instance requirement
- An abstraction extracted after exactly one occurrence

---

# Part 3: Systematic Debugging

**Owns:** root-causing any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior before
attempting a fix.

## The Iron Law

```
NO FIXES WITHOUT ROOT CAUSE INVESTIGATION FIRST
```

Use for ANY technical issue — test failures, production bugs, unexpected
behavior, performance problems, build failures, integration issues.
Especially under time pressure, when "just one quick fix" seems obvious,
after previous fixes didn't work, or when the issue seems simple (simple
bugs have root causes too).

## Phase 1: Root Cause Investigation

**Before attempting any fix:**

1. **Read error messages carefully** — stack traces completely, line
   numbers, file paths, error codes. They often contain the exact solution.
2. **Reproduce consistently** — can you trigger it reliably? If not
   reproducible, gather more data, don't guess.
3. **Check recent changes** — git diff, recent commits, new dependencies,
   config/environment differences.
4. **Gather evidence in multi-component systems** — add diagnostic
   instrumentation at each component boundary (log what enters/exits, verify
   config propagation, check state at each layer) *before* proposing
   fixes. Run once to see WHERE it breaks, then investigate that specific
   component.
5. **Trace data flow backward** — where does the bad value originate? What
   called this with it? Keep tracing up until you find the source. Fix at
   the source, not the symptom.

## Phase 2: Pattern Analysis

1. **Find working examples** — locate similar working code in the same
   codebase.
2. **Compare against references** — read a reference implementation
   completely, not skimmed, before applying its pattern.
3. **Identify every difference** between working and broken — don't
   assume "that can't matter."
4. **Understand dependencies** — what components, settings, environment,
   assumptions does this need?

## Phase 3: Hypothesis and Testing

1. **Form a single hypothesis** — "I think X is the root cause because Y." Write it down, be specific.
2. **Test minimally** — smallest possible change, one variable at a time.
3. **Verify before continuing** — worked → Phase 4. Didn't → new
   hypothesis, don't stack another fix on top.
4. **When you don't know** — say "I don't understand X." Don't pretend.

## Phase 4: Implementation

1. **Create a failing test case first** — simplest reproduction, automated
   if possible. Must exist before fixing.
2. **Implement a single fix** — address the root cause, one change, no
   "while I'm here" improvements, no bundled refactoring.
3. **Verify the fix** — test passes now, no other tests broken, issue
   actually resolved.
4. **If the fix doesn't work** — STOP. Count attempts. Under 3 → return to
   Phase 1 with new information. **3 or more → question the architecture
   (step 5), don't attempt fix #4.**
5. **3+ failed fixes = architectural problem, not a failed hypothesis.**
   Pattern: each fix reveals new shared state/coupling elsewhere, each
   requires "massive refactoring," each creates new symptoms elsewhere.
   Stop and question whether the pattern is fundamentally sound before
   attempting more fixes. This may route back to Part 1 for a genuine
   redesign discussion.

## Worked example: a "mysterious" exit code

Concrete anchor for the phases above. A Docker build failed on
`adduser --system --uid 1001 --gid app --no-create-home app` with
`exit code: 51` and a full dump of `adduser --help`.

- **Phase 1, read the error carefully** — 51 is not arbitrary: the adduser man
  page defines it ("Incorrect number or order of command line parameters
  detected"). The usage dump meant argument parsing, not filesystem failure.
- **Phase 1, check recent changes** — an earlier guess (removing
  `--disabled-password`) had changed nothing; treat that as unresolved, not as a
  known fix. Investigate again rather than stacking another edit.
- **Phase 2, find working examples** — official Debian-slim images
  (node:*-slim) create their non-root user with `groupadd`/`useradd`, never
  `adduser`; an sbt-native-packager issue showed the identical exit 51 from a
  malformed `adduser` invocation on Debian trixie.
- **Phase 3, single hypothesis** — the rewritten Debian `adduser` accepts only a
  numeric ID for `--gid`; the name variant is `--ingroup`. `--gid app` violates
  that. **Test minimally** — switch to the proven shadow-utils idiom
  `groupadd --system --gid 1001 app && useradd --system --uid 1001 --gid app
  --no-create-home app`.
- **Phase 4, verify** — rebuild clean; no bundled refactors, no second fix.

## Red Flags (Part 3)

Catch yourself thinking any of these → STOP, return to Phase 1:

- "Quick fix for now, investigate later"
- "Just try changing X and see if it works"
- "Add multiple changes, run tests"
- "Skip the test, I'll manually verify"
- "It's probably X, let me fix that"
- "I don't fully understand but this might work"
- "One more fix attempt" (when already tried 2+)
- Each fix reveals a new problem in a different place
- Proposing solutions before tracing data flow

## Common Rationalizations (Part 3)

| Excuse | Reality |
|---|---|
| "Issue is simple, don't need process" | Simple issues have root causes too. |
| "Emergency, no time for process" | Systematic debugging is faster than guess-and-check thrashing. |
| "Just try this first, then investigate" | The first fix sets the pattern — do it right from the start. |
| "Multiple fixes at once saves time" | Can't isolate what worked; causes new bugs. |
| "I see the problem, let me fix it" | Seeing symptoms ≠ understanding root cause. |

## When No Root Cause Is Found

If systematic investigation reveals the issue is truly environmental,
timing-dependent, or external: document what was investigated, implement
appropriate handling (retry, timeout, error message), add monitoring for
future investigation. But 95% of "no root cause" cases are incomplete
investigation — make sure Phase 1 was actually exhausted first.

---

# Part 4: Code Simplification

**Owns:** reducing complexity in code that already works, without changing
behavior.

## When to Use

After a feature works but feels heavier than it needs to be; during review
when readability/complexity is flagged; deeply nested logic, long
functions, unclear names; refactoring code written under time pressure;
consolidating logic scattered across files.

**When not to:** code is already clean (don't simplify for its own sake);
you don't yet understand what the code does (comprehend first); the code
is performance-critical and the simpler version would be measurably
slower; you're about to rewrite the module entirely.

## The Test

Every simplification must pass: **"Would a new team member understand
this faster than the original?"**

## The Five Principles

### 1. Preserve Behavior Exactly

All inputs, outputs, side effects, error behavior, and edge cases stay
identical. Before every change: does this produce the same output for
every input? Same error behavior? Same side effects and ordering? Do
existing tests still pass without modification?

### 2. Follow Project Conventions

Simplification means matching the codebase's own conventions, not
imposing external preferences: import ordering, function declaration
style, naming, error handling, type annotation depth. Simplification that
breaks project consistency is churn, not simplification.

### 3. Prefer Clarity Over Cleverness

```typescript
// UNCLEAR: dense ternary chain
const label = isNew ? 'New' : isUpdated ? 'Updated' : isArchived ? 'Archived' : 'Active';

// CLEAR: readable mapping
function getStatusLabel(item: Item): string {
  if (item.isNew) return 'New';
  if (item.isUpdated) return 'Updated';
  if (item.isArchived) return 'Archived';
  return 'Active';
}
```

### 4. Maintain Balance — Watch for Over-Simplification

- Inlining too aggressively — removing a helper that named a concept makes the call site harder to read
- Combining unrelated logic — two simple functions merged into one complex one is not simpler
- Removing "unnecessary" abstraction that actually exists for extensibility or testability
- Optimizing for line count instead of comprehension speed

### 5. Scope to What Changed

Default to simplifying recently modified code. Avoid drive-by refactors of
unrelated code unless explicitly asked. Unscoped simplification creates
noisy diffs and risks unintended regressions.

## The Process

1. **Understand before touching (Chesterton's Fence)** — what is this
   code's responsibility, what calls it, what are the edge cases, are
   there tests defining expected behavior, why might it have been written
   this way? Check git blame. If you can't answer these, read more before
   simplifying.

2. **Identify opportunities** — concrete signals, not vague smells:
   - **Structural:** deep nesting (3+ levels) → guard clauses; long
     functions (50+ lines) → split by responsibility; nested ternaries →
     if/else or lookup objects; boolean parameter flags → options objects
     or separate functions; repeated conditionals → extract a predicate
   - **Naming:** generic names (`data`, `temp`, `val`) → describe the
     content; misleading names (a `get` that mutates) → rename to reflect
     behavior; comments explaining "what" → delete (code should speak for
     itself); comments explaining "why" → keep, they carry intent
   - **Redundancy:** duplicated 5+ line blocks → extract shared function
     (see Part 2's Rule of Three); dead code → remove after confirming
     it's truly dead; unnecessary wrapper abstractions → inline; redundant
     type assertions → remove

3. **Apply incrementally** — one simplification at a time, run tests after
   each. If tests pass, continue or commit. If tests fail, revert and
   reconsider. Never batch multiple untested simplifications. **The Rule
   of 500:** a refactor touching 500+ lines needs automation (codemods,
   scripts), not manual edits.

4. **Verify the result** — is the simplified version genuinely easier to
   understand? Any new patterns inconsistent with the codebase? Is the
   diff clean and reviewable? Would a teammate approve it? If harder to
   understand, revert.

## Language-Specific Patterns

```python
# SIMPLIFY: nested conditionals → early return
# Before
def process(data):
    if data is not None:
        if data.is_valid():
            if data.has_permission():
                return do_work(data)
            else:
                raise PermissionError("No permission")
        else:
            raise ValueError("Invalid data")
    else:
        raise TypeError("Data is None")

# After
def process(data):
    if data is None:
        raise TypeError("Data is None")
    if not data.is_valid():
        raise ValueError("Invalid data")
    if not data.has_permission():
        raise PermissionError("No permission")
    return do_work(data)
```

```typescript
// SIMPLIFY: unnecessary async wrapper
// Before: async function getUser(id) { return await userService.findById(id); }
// After:  function getUser(id) { return userService.findById(id); }

// SIMPLIFY: manual array building → filter
// Before: for (const user of users) { if (user.isActive) activeUsers.push(user); }
// After:  const activeUsers = users.filter((user) => user.isActive);
```

```c
/* SIMPLIFY: nested conditionals → early return (guard clauses) */
/* Before */
int process(Data *data) {
    if (data != NULL) {
        if (is_valid(data)) {
            if (has_permission(data)) {
                return do_work(data);
            } else {
                return ERR_PERMISSION;
            }
        } else {
            return ERR_INVALID;
        }
    } else {
        return ERR_NULL;
    }
}

/* After */
int process(Data *data) {
    if (data == NULL) return ERR_NULL;
    if (!is_valid(data)) return ERR_INVALID;
    if (!has_permission(data)) return ERR_PERMISSION;
    return do_work(data);
}
```

```cpp
// SIMPLIFY: manual owning pointer + manual delete → RAII / smart pointer
// Before: leaks on every early return, and on exceptions
Widget* makeWidget() {
    Widget* w = new Widget();
    if (!w->init()) { delete w; return nullptr; }  // easy to forget
    return w;
}

// After: ownership is automatic, no manual delete, exception-safe
std::unique_ptr<Widget> makeWidget() {
    auto w = std::make_unique<Widget>();
    if (!w->init()) return nullptr;   // destructor runs automatically
    return w;
}

// SIMPLIFY: raw loop with index bookkeeping → range-based for
// Before: for (size_t i = 0; i < items.size(); ++i) { process(items[i]); }
// After:  for (const auto& item : items) { process(item); }
```

**C/C++-specific simplification signals** (in addition to the structural/
naming/redundancy signals above): manual `malloc`/`free` or `new`/`delete`
pairs that could be RAII (`unique_ptr`, `vector`, a destructor) — the
"redundancy" and "dead code" categories above apply doubly here, since a
missed `free` is a leak, not just clutter; raw pointers used for ownership
where a smart pointer or reference would make the ownership explicit;
macros used where a `const`/`constexpr`/inline function would be clearer
and type-safe; C-style casts (`(int)x`) where `static_cast`/`const_cast`
would make the intent explicit.

## Common Rationalizations (Part 4)

| Rationalization | Reality |
|---|---|
| "It's working, no need to touch it" | Hard-to-read working code will be hard to fix when it breaks. |
| "Fewer lines is always simpler" | A 1-line nested ternary isn't simpler than a 5-line if/else. |
| "I'll simplify this unrelated code too" | Unscoped simplification creates noisy diffs and risks regressions. Stay focused. |
| "This abstraction might be useful later" | Speculative abstraction is complexity without value (see Part 2 YAGNI). Remove it, re-add when needed. |
| "I'll refactor while adding this feature" | Separate refactoring from feature work — mixed changes are harder to review and revert. |

## Red Flags (Part 4)

- Simplification that requires modifying tests to pass (behavior likely changed)
- "Simplified" code that's longer and harder to follow than the original
- Renaming to match personal preference rather than project conventions
- Removing error handling because "it's cleaner"
- Simplifying code you don't fully understand
- Batching many simplifications into one large, hard-to-review commit

---

# Part 5: Code Review & Quality

**Owns:** the merge gate. Every change passes through here regardless of
which earlier part(s) it came from.

**Approval standard:** approve when a change definitely improves overall
code health, even if imperfect. Don't block because it isn't exactly how
you'd have written it.

## The Five-Axis Review

### 1. Correctness
Matches spec/requirements? Edge cases handled (null, empty, boundary)?
Error paths handled, not just the happy path? Tests actually test the
right things? Any off-by-one errors, race conditions, state
inconsistencies?

### 2. Readability & Simplicity
Names descriptive and consistent with conventions? Control flow
straightforward? Organized logically? Any "clever" tricks that should be
simplified (see Part 4)? Could this be fewer lines without losing clarity?
Are abstractions earning their complexity (see Part 2 YAGNI — don't
generalize until the third use case)? Dead code artifacts (`_unused`
variables, backwards-compat shims, `// removed` comments)? Is a new
conditional bolted onto an unrelated flow (design smell — push into its
own helper, not tangled into an existing path)? Do repeated conditionals
on the same shape appear (signals a missing model — see Part 2's GoF
table)?

### 3. Architecture
Follows existing patterns, or is a new one justified? Clean module
boundaries? Duplication that should be shared (Part 2's DRY test)?
Dependencies flowing the right direction? Appropriate abstraction level?
**Does this refactor reduce complexity or just relocate it?** — count the
concepts a reader must hold; prefer restructuring that makes branches/
modes/layers disappear over one that re-centralizes the same logic. Is
feature-specific logic leaking into a shared module? Are type boundaries
explicit — question gratuitous `any`/`unknown`/casts and silent fallbacks
that paper over an unclear invariant.

### 4. Security
Input validated/sanitized? Secrets kept out of code, logs, VCS?
Authentication/authorization checked where needed? SQL parameterized (no
string concatenation)? Outputs encoded against XSS? Dependencies trusted,
no known vulnerabilities? External data (APIs, logs, user content, config)
treated as untrusted and validated at system boundaries?

### 5. Performance
N+1 query patterns? Unbounded loops or unconstrained data fetching?
Synchronous operations that should be async? Unnecessary UI re-renders?
Missing pagination on list endpoints? Large objects created in hot paths?

## Structural Remedies

Propose the move, not just the problem:

- Replace a chain of conditionals with a typed model or explicit dispatcher
- Collapse duplicate branches into one clearer flow
- Separate orchestration from business logic
- Move feature-specific logic out of a shared module into the package that owns the concept
- Reuse the canonical helper instead of a bespoke near-duplicate
- Make a type boundary explicit so downstream branching disappears
- Delete a pass-through wrapper that adds indirection without clarifying the API
- Extract a helper, or split a large file into focused modules

(For pattern-shaped remedies specifically — a chain of conditionals that
wants to become Strategy, a creation mess that wants to become Factory —
consult Part 2's GoF Pattern Decision Table.)

Prefer the remedy that removes moving pieces over one that spreads the
same complexity around.

## Change Sizing

```
~100 lines changed   → Good, reviewable in one sitting
~300 lines changed   → Acceptable if a single logical change
~1000 lines changed  → Too large, split it
```

Watch file size too, not just diff size — a small diff can push a file
past ~1000 total lines, a common inspection signal. When a change
materially grows an already-large file, decompose first, then add.

**One change** = a single self-contained modification addressing one
thing, with related tests, keeping the system functional after
submission — one part of a feature, not the whole thing.

**Splitting strategies:** Stack (small sequential changes), By file group
(separate reviewers for cross-cutting concerns), Horizontal (shared
code/stubs first, then consumers), Vertical (smaller full-stack feature
slices).

**Separate refactoring from feature work** — a change that refactors and
adds behavior is two changes, submit separately.

## Change Descriptions

**First line:** short, imperative, standalone — "Delete the FizzBuzz RPC"
not "Deleting the FizzBuzz RPC." Someone searching history should
understand the change without reading the diff.

**Body:** what's changing and why — context, decisions, reasoning not
visible in the code. Link bugs/benchmarks/design docs. Acknowledge
shortcomings.

**Avoid:** "Fix bug," "Fix build," "Add patch," "Moving code from A to B," "Phase 1."

## Review Process

1. **Understand the context** — what is this trying to accomplish, what
   spec/task does it implement, what's the expected behavior change?
2. **Review tests first** — do they exist, test behavior not
   implementation, cover edge cases, have descriptive names, would they
   catch a regression?
3. **Review implementation** against all five axes above, file by file.
4. **Categorize findings** by severity:

| Prefix | Meaning | Author Action |
|---|---|---|
| *(no prefix)* | Required change | Must address before merge |
| **Critical:** | Blocks merge | Security, data loss, broken functionality |
| **Nit:** | Minor, optional | May ignore |
| **Optional:/Consider:** | Suggestion | Worth considering, not required |
| **FYI** | Informational | No action needed |

**Lead with what matters** — correctness and security first, then
structural regressions and missed simplifications, then everything else.
One structural problem beats ten nits for attention.

5. **Verify the verification** — what tests were run, did the build pass,
   was it tested manually, screenshots for UI changes, before/after
   comparison?

## Dead Code Hygiene

After any refactor: identify unreachable/unused code, list it explicitly,
**ask before deleting** — "Should I remove these now-unused elements:
[list]?" Don't silently delete things you're not sure about.

## Handling Disagreements

Hierarchy: (1) technical facts/data override opinions, (2) style guides
are absolute authority on style, (3) software design evaluated on
engineering principles not personal preference, (4) codebase consistency
acceptable if it doesn't degrade overall health.

**Don't accept "I'll clean it up later."** Require cleanup before
submission unless a genuine emergency; otherwise require filing a bug with
self-assignment.

## Honesty in Review

Don't rubber-stamp ("LGTM" with no evidence of review). Don't soften real
issues. Quantify problems when possible ("this N+1 will add ~50ms per item"
beats "this could be slow"). Push back on approaches with clear problems —
sycophancy is a review failure mode. Accept override gracefully if the
author has full context and disagrees; comment on code, not people.

## Dependency Discipline

**Before adding any dependency:** does the existing stack already solve
this? How large is it? Actively maintained? Known vulnerabilities
(`npm audit`)? License compatible? Prefer standard library and existing
utilities — every dependency is a liability.

**Upgrading a dependency** is a code change like any other:
1. Read the changelog, not just the version number — semver doesn't
   guarantee no breakage
2. One dependency per change — a bulk bump that breaks the build hides
   which package did it
3. Let tests decide — verified by a green suite before *and* after; thin
   coverage around the dependency's behavior is itself a finding
4. Mind the transitive graph — review the lockfile diff, not just the
   manifest
5. Keep the lockfile honest — commit it, review its diff, never hand-edit it

## The Review Checklist

```markdown
### Correctness
- [ ] Matches spec/task requirements
- [ ] Edge cases handled
- [ ] Error paths handled
- [ ] Tests cover the change adequately

### Readability
- [ ] Names are clear and consistent
- [ ] Logic is straightforward
- [ ] No unnecessary complexity

### Architecture
- [ ] Follows existing patterns
- [ ] No unnecessary coupling or dependencies
- [ ] Refactors reduce complexity rather than relocate it
- [ ] No feature logic in shared modules; file stays within a healthy size

### Security
- [ ] No secrets in code
- [ ] Input validated at boundaries
- [ ] No injection vulnerabilities
- [ ] Auth checks in place
- [ ] External data sources treated as untrusted

### Performance
- [ ] No N+1 patterns
- [ ] No unbounded operations
- [ ] Pagination on list endpoints

### Verification
- [ ] Tests pass
- [ ] Build succeeds
- [ ] Manual verification done (if applicable)

### Verdict
- [ ] Approve — ready to merge
- [ ] Request changes — issues must be addressed
```

## Common Rationalizations (Part 5)

| Rationalization | Reality |
|---|---|
| "It works, that's good enough" | Unreadable, insecure, or architecturally wrong code creates compounding debt. |
| "I wrote it, so I know it's correct" | Authors are blind to their own assumptions. |
| "We'll clean it up later" | Later never comes — require cleanup before merge. |
| "The tests pass, so it's good" | Tests don't catch architecture, security, or readability problems. |
| "The refactor makes it cleaner" | Relocating complexity isn't reducing it — look for the version where branches disappear. |
| "It's just a version bump" | A bump is a behavior change you didn't write. Read the changelog. |

## Red Flags (Part 5)

- PRs merged without any review
- Review that only checks if tests pass
- "LGTM" without evidence of actual review
- Security-sensitive changes without security-focused review
- Large PRs "too big to review properly" (split them)
- No regression tests with bug fix PRs
- Review comments without severity labels
- A refactor that moves code around without reducing the concepts a reader must hold
- A change that grows an already-large file instead of decomposing it
- New conditionals scattered into unrelated code paths
- A bespoke helper duplicating an existing canonical one
- A bulk "bump dependencies" PR with no changelog review

---

## Final Verification (all parts)

Before any change is considered done:

- [ ] A design was proposed and approved before implementation started (Part 1)
- [ ] Each class/module can be described without using "and" (Part 2, SRP)
- [ ] Adding a plausible new case means adding code, not editing an existing chain (Part 2, OCP)
- [ ] No `isinstance` checks needed by callers to use a type safely (Part 2, LSP)
- [ ] Duplicated-looking code passed the "same knowledge" test (Part 2, DRY)
- [ ] The duplication-prevention search was actually run before new code was written (Part 2)
- [ ] Every design pattern in use maps to a real symptom, not just a familiar name (Part 2, GoF)
- [ ] Any bug was root-caused, not just patched at the symptom (Part 3)
- [ ] A failing test existed before the fix (Part 3)
- [ ] Simplification (if any) preserved behavior exactly and passed all existing tests unmodified (Part 4)
- [ ] The change passed the five-axis review and all Critical/Required findings are resolved (Part 5)
- [ ] The verification story is documented: what changed, how it was verified
